questions.question:
Write a function countPairs(n) with:
 for i from 1 to n:
   for j from 1 to floor(n/i):
     // O(1) work
What is the overall time complexity and space usage? Provide the tight bound, discuss best/worst-case, and compare to a memory-efficient variant that avoids the inner loop entirely?

questions.explanation:
## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to derive nested-loop complexity where inner bound shrinks with i, revealing a harmonic-sum growth.

## Key Concepts
- Harmonic series: sum floor(n/i) ~ n log n
- Theta vs O, space O(1)
- Closed-form approximations vs exact sums

## Code Example
```javascript
function countPairs(n){
  let count=0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let j=1;j<=Math.floor(n/i);j++){
      count++;
    }
  }
  return count;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you prove the Theta(n log n) bound?
- How does changing inner bound to floor(n/i^2) affect complexity?
- What are practical implications for large n?

## Why This Is Asked
Understanding how to count inversions online with limited memory and batched updates tests both data structures and amortized reasoning.

## Key Concepts
- Fenwick tree for prefix sums; online per-element updates
- Inversion counting formula: add current_seen - count_of_≤v
- Amortized batch analysis and memory scaling with M

## Code Example
```javascript
class Fenwick {
  constructor(n){ this.f=[0]; for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) this.f[i]=0; }
  add(i,delta){ for(i+=1;i<this.f.length;i+=i&-i) this.f[i]+=delta }
  sum(i){ let s=0; for(i+=1;i>0;i-=i&-i) s+=this.f[i]; return s }
}
function processBatch(batch, M, fen, totalSeen){ let inversions=0; for(const v of batch){ inversions += totalSeen - fen.sum(v); fen.add(v,1); totalSeen++; } return {inversions, totalSeen}; }
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle 64-bit inversion counts on large streams?
- Compare to using a segment tree; when is it advantageous?

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to design an incremental data structure for computing a non-trivial graph statistic under streaming batch updates.

## Key Concepts
- Incremental degree-based metrics
- Amortized analysis for batch/streaming updates
- Space-time trade-offs in dynamic graph algorithms

## Code Example
```javascript
let deg = Array(n).fill(0);
let P = 0;

function addEdge(u, v) {
  const du = deg[u], dv = deg[v];
  P += 2 * (du + dv);
  deg[u] = du + 1;
  deg[v] = dv + 1;
}

function processBatch(edges) {
  edges.forEach(([u, v]) => addEdge(u, v));
  return P;
}
```

